## How did we learn this?
This came from 2 sources. In 2024, we had issues with our intake, namely our bream break kept being smashed or unplugged. Although we did want to just have it be better crimp and maybe create a casing for it, we at the time, did not like relying on the other buckets to get everything right (which to be fair, they had a history of forgetting the small details and then forcing us to make it work or be manageable). So, we decided to start looking for other avenues and one idea that caught our attention was seeing a Behind-The-Bumpers (I forgot which one) where another team mentioned using current to detect if a game piece was present. This led to looking into current sensing which we found in a cheifdelphi article. 

## Applications in FRC
- Game Piece Detection: Detect when a ball, cone, or other game piece is picked up.
- Jam Detection: Identify when a mechanism is jammed and prevent motor damage by stopping or reversing the motor.
- Automated Scoring: Use current sensing to confirm when a game piece is scored or released.

## Considerations
- Calibration: You'll need to calibrate the system to account for normal variations in current draw during operation.
- Noise Filtering: Sudden spikes or noise in the current data may require filtering to avoid false positives.
- Sensor Placement: Ensure the current sensor is monitoring the correct motor circuit for accurate detection.
